    "breadcrumb": "Core Design \u003e Threads",
    "content": "Working draft for faculty review. A spiral deepens through escalating passes across the two-year core. This is the spiral; one of eight spirals (with three lenses and one bounded practice — twelve threads total, plus two cross-cutting norms).\nIn one line: Understanding programs — your own and others’ — from ‘make it work’ to ‘defend it under scrutiny.’\nCharacter The lens shifts each pass rather than just raising difficulty: produce → understand others’ → organize → reason about → defend.\nPasses across the two years Course What’s new at this pass CIS 116 (Y1 S1) — Introduction to Programming Write small correct programs across the paradigms it introduces (imperative and functional, with the same widgets carried forward from the original design); “make it work.” Also a first, informal comprehension touch in the same course — SLO 4 is explicitly “read, interpret, and modify existing computer programs,” ahead of CIS 200’s formal Code Archaeology pass. CIS 200 (Y1 S2) — Programming Fundamentals Read others’ code, explain unfamiliar behavior — the thread’s signature “Code Archaeology” assessment; “understand before judging.” Encapsulation/abstraction as comprehension tools (tied to the course’s OOP introduction) and functional-pattern formalization (map/filter/reduce): organize so intent is legible. CIS 300 (Y2 S1) - Data Structures and Algorithms Explain data structure manipulations. What is the code doing to transform data structures and their memory state. CIS 301 (Y2 S1) — Logical Foundations of Programming Reason systematically about whether code does what it should — this course’s own confirmed purpose is deepening propositional/predicate logic into program verification, a natural extension of comprehension into formal reasoning. CIS 400 (Y2 S2) — capstone of the core Defend your understanding and choices under scrutiny — a live walkthrough / architecture defense is the course’s actual assessment mechanism, not spec-compliance alone. Connections Code Archaeology (CIS 200) is the signature assessment this thread builds toward. Adjacent to Correctness \u0026 Verification (reasoning about code) and Professional Practices (documentation as explanation).",
    "description": "Working draft for faculty review. A spiral deepens through escalating passes across the two-year core. This is the spiral; one of eight spirals (with three lenses and one bounded practice — twelve threads total, plus two cross-cutting norms).\nIn one line: Understanding programs — your own and others’ — from ‘make it work’ to ‘defend it under scrutiny.’\nCharacter The lens shifts each pass rather than just raising difficulty: produce → understand others’ → organize → reason about → defend.",
