    "content": "Full citations for sources referenced throughout this site, in IEEE style, alphabetical by first author. Each entry has a stable anchor; in-text citations elsewhere on the site link here by name (e.g. #bruner-1960), not by number, so the list can grow without breaking existing links.\nAkkerman \u0026 Bakker, 2011 S. F. Akkerman and A. Bakker, “Boundary crossing and boundary objects,” Review of Educational Research, vol. 81, no. 2, pp. 132–169, 2011.\nBego et al., 2024 C. R. Bego, K. B. Lyle, P. A. S. Ralston, J. C. Immekus, R. J. Chastain, L. D. Haynes, L. K. Hoyt, R. M. Pigg, S. D. Rabin, M. W. Scobee, and T. L. Starr, “Single-paper meta-analyses of the effects of spaced retrieval practice in nine introductory STEM courses: is the glass half full or half empty?” International Journal of STEM Education, vol. 11, 2024, doi: 10.1186/s40594-024-00468-5.\nBruner, 1960 J. S. Bruner, The Process of Education. Cambridge, MA, USA: Harvard University Press, 1960.\nCAST, 2024 CAST, Universal Design for Learning Guidelines, version 3.0. Lynnfield, MA, USA: CAST, 2024.\nCepeda et al., 2006 N. J. Cepeda, H. Pashler, E. Vul, J. T. Wixted, and D. Rohrer, “Distributed practice in verbal recall tasks: A review and quantitative synthesis,” Psychological Bulletin, vol. 132, no. 3, pp. 354–380, 2006.\nCollins et al., 1989 A. Collins, J. S. Brown, and S. E. Newman, “Cognitive apprenticeship: Teaching the crafts of reading, writing, and mathematics,” in Knowing, Learning, and Instruction: Essays in Honor of Robert Glaser, L. B. Resnick, Ed. Hillsdale, NJ, USA: Lawrence Erlbaum Associates, 1989, pp. 453–494.\nDunton \u0026 Co, 2019 J. B. Dunton and W. S. Co, “Spiral progression approach in teaching science and the performance of learners,” Journal of Physics: Conference Series, vol. 1254, Art. no. 012045, 2019.\nFraser et al., 2019 S. Fraser, A. D. Wright, P. van Donkelaar, and J. D. Smirl, “Cross-sectional comparison of spiral versus block integrated curriculums in preparing medical students to diagnose and manage concussions,” BMC Medical Education, vol. 19, Art. no. 17, 2019.\nHarden \u0026 Stamper, 1999 R. M. Harden and N. Stamper, “What is a spiral curriculum?” Medical Teacher, vol. 21, no. 2, pp. 141–143, 1999.\nIreland \u0026 Mouthaan, 2020 J. Ireland and M. Mouthaan, “Perspectives on curriculum design: comparing the spiral and the network models,” Research Matters, no. 30, pp. 7–12, Autumn 2020.\nIsrael et al., 2017 M. Israel, T. Lash, and M. Ray, “Universal Design for Learning within Computer Science Education,” Creative Technology Research Lab, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, Champaign, IL, USA, 2017.\nJohnston, 2012 H. Johnston, “The Spiral Curriculum,” Research into Practice, Education Partnerships, Inc., Tempe, AZ, USA, 2012.\nLave \u0026 Wenger, 1991 J. Lave and E. Wenger, Situated Learning: Legitimate Peripheral Participation. Cambridge, UK: Cambridge University Press, 1991.\nLionelle et al., 2022 A. Lionelle, S. Ghosh, B. Say, and J. R. Beveridge, “Increase performance in CS 2 via a spiral redesign of CS 1,” in Proc. 53rd ACM Tech. Symp. on Computer Science Education (SIGCSE 2022), New York, NY, USA, 2022, pp. 502–508.\nMaltagliati et al., 2023 A. J. Maltagliati, J. H. Paree, K. L. McIntosh, K. F. Moynahan, and T. W. Vanderah, “Development and evaluation of a pre-clerkship spiral curriculum: data from three medical school classes,” Medical Education Online, vol. 28, no. 1, Art. no. 2167258, 2023.\nMislevy et al., 2003 R. J. Mislevy, R. G. Almond, and J. F. Lukas, “A Brief Introduction to Evidence-Centered Design,” Educational Testing Service, Princeton, NJ, USA, ETS Res. Rep. RR-03-16, 2003.\nMislevy \u0026 Haertel, 2006 R. J. Mislevy and G. D. Haertel, “Implications of evidence-centered design for educational testing,” Educational Measurement: Issues and Practice, vol. 25, no. 4, pp. 6–20, 2006.\nMislevy et al., 2003 R. J. Mislevy, L. S. Steinberg, and R. G. Almond, “On the structure of educational assessments,” Measurement: Interdisciplinary Research and Perspectives, vol. 1, no. 1, pp. 3–62, 2003.\nRose \u0026 Meyer, 2002 D. H. Rose and A. Meyer, Teaching Every Student in the Digital Age: Universal Design for Learning. Alexandria, VA, USA: ASCD, 2002.\nStar \u0026 Griesemer, 1989 S. L. Star and J. R. Griesemer, “Institutional ecology, ’translations,’ and boundary objects: Amateurs and professionals in Berkeley’s Museum of Vertebrate Zoology, 1907–39,” Social Studies of Science, vol. 19, no. 3, pp. 387–420, 1989.\nWenger, 1998 E. Wenger, Communities of Practice: Learning, Meaning, and Identity. Cambridge, UK: Cambridge University Press, 1998.\nYost et al., 2008 S. A. Yost et al., “Development of an integrated spiral curriculum in electrical and computer engineering,” in Proc. 2008 American Society for Engineering Education Annual Conf. \u0026 Exposition, Paper AC 2008-210, 2008.",
