    "breadcrumb": "Catalog \u003e Courses",
    "content": "Course Description Survey of computer networking fundamentals, including 7-layer OSI model, common networking protocols, socket-based network programming, network security and encryption, and network performance and reliability basics.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview This course will introduce students to the core concepts of network communication between computing systems. Students will learn about the 7-layer OSI network model and how common networking protocols such as TCP/IP, HTTP, DHCP, DNS, and more work to form the foundation of the modern internet. Students will explore basic socket-based network programming, discuss security and encryption of network communications, and explore concepts related to network performance and reliability.\nCourse Goals and Learning Outcomes Explain the 7-level OSI model, and discuss where various protocols sit within that model Describe how data is encapsulated into packets for transmission Construct a working socket-based networked program such as a chat server Differentiate between common networking protocols Assess security vulnerabilities and analyze the security of a network connection Describe the distributed structure of the internet Examine network performance and reliability metrics Course Prerequisites None/varies by topic. Instructor permission required.\nRequired Textbooks and Equipment TBD\nGrading Grading Modality: Graded (A, B, C, D, F, I)\nCourse Schedule TBD\nAssignments TBD",
    "description": "Course Description Survey of computer networking fundamentals, including 7-layer OSI model, common networking protocols, socket-based network programming, network security and encryption, and network performance and reliability basics.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview This course will introduce students to the core concepts of network communication between computing systems. Students will learn about the 7-layer OSI network model and how common networking protocols such as TCP/IP, HTTP, DHCP, DNS, and more work to form the foundation of the modern internet. Students will explore basic socket-based network programming, discuss security and encryption of network communications, and explore concepts related to network performance and reliability.",
