    "breadcrumb": "Catalog \u003e Courses",
    "content": "Course Description This course introduces the fundamental concepts of cybersecurity, including how digital systems communicate, common cyber threats, security principles, risk management, ethical considerations, and career opportunities in the field. Students will develop a cybersecurity mindset through case studies, discussions, and hands-on activities.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview This course serves as a basic introduction to cybersecurity with a focus on exploring common threats and mitigations. Students will learn key principles such as the CIA triad, authentication, authorization, encryption, trust, and more. The course will include case studies of common cybersecurity threats and incidents and discussions on how to prevent them in the future. Students should leave the course with a defensive mindset and an understanding of risks and vulnerabilities present in modern technology.\nCourse Goals and Learning Outcomes Upon successful completion of the course, students will be able to:\nExplain the importance of cybersecurity in modern society. Describe key cybersecurity principles, including the CIA Triad. Identify common cyber threats and attack methods. Analyze cybersecurity risks and potential impacts. Describe defensive security measures and best practices. Discuss legal and ethical issues in cybersecurity. Evaluate cybersecurity incidents and recommend mitigations. Course Prerequisites None/varies by topic. Instructor permission required.\nRequired Textbooksk and Equipment TBD\nGrading Grading Modality: Graded (A, B, C, D, F, I)\nCourse Schedule Week Topic Major Concepts Assignment 1 Introduction to Cybersecurity Security mindset, cybercrime, digital assets, cybersecurity domains Cybersecurity Reflection 2 Data, Information, and Security Principles CIA Triad, authentication, authorization, risk CIA Triad Analysis 3 Networks and the Internet IP addresses, DNS, packets, network communication Network Mapping Activity 4 Threats and Attackers Malware, phishing, social engineering, insider threats Threat Research Brief 5 Protecting Systems and Data Passwords, MFA, encryption, backups, updates Personal Security Audit 6 Risk Management and Incident Response Risk assessment, vulnerabilities, response planning Risk Assessment Worksheet 7 Cyber Ethics, Privacy, and Law Ethics, privacy, regulations, responsible behavior Cyber Ethics Discussion 8 Emerging Technologies and Careers AI, cloud security, IoT, cybersecurity careers Final Presentation Assignments Overview TBD\nProposed Changes Add MATH Recursive and Modular Computation (Y1 Spring) as a formal prerequisite. resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md’s own CIS 251 entry says this course’s applied-cryptography content builds on that course’s modular arithmetic, “correctly sequenced before this course” — but this page’s Course Prerequisites section currently lists none (“None/varies by topic. Instructor permission required.”). Surfaced during the 2026-08-04 internal-prerequisite-analysis regeneration; logged here as a proposed addition rather than asserted as already required, pending a course-designer/faculty decision on whether to formalize it.",
    "description": "Course Description This course introduces the fundamental concepts of cybersecurity, including how digital systems communicate, common cyber threats, security principles, risk management, ethical considerations, and career opportunities in the field. Students will develop a cybersecurity mindset through case studies, discussions, and hands-on activities.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview This course serves as a basic introduction to cybersecurity with a focus on exploring common threats and mitigations. Students will learn key principles such as the CIA triad, authentication, authorization, encryption, trust, and more. The course will include case studies of common cybersecurity threats and incidents and discussions on how to prevent them in the future. Students should leave the course with a defensive mindset and an understanding of risks and vulnerabilities present in modern technology.",
