    "breadcrumb": "Catalog \u003e Courses",
    "content": "Course Description This 1‑credit hour course introduces students to Structured Query Language (SQL) for retrieving and analyzing data in relational databases. Students will learn to write basic queries to select, filter, summarize, and combine data from multiple tables using hands‑on exercises and real‑world examples.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview The objective of this course is to provide students with the foundations to understand simple relational databases. With this knowledge, students will be able to understand and write SQL queries to find, organize, and summarize information from real-world datasets.\nCourse Goals and Learning Outcomes Explain the purpose of SQL and how relational databases are used to store and retrieve structured data. Write basic SQL queries using (but not limited to) SELECT, FROM, and WHERE to retrieve specific data from a relational database. Sort and filter query results using ORDER BY, comparison operators, and logical operators. Summarize data using aggregate functions, such as COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, with GROUP BY. Combine data from multiple tables using basic JOIN operations. Write SQL queries that modify data in an existing database, such as INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE Use programmable objects to create meaningful transformations of data, such as VIEWS and table expressions. Employ advanced SQL techniques, like sub queries, to answer more complex questions Interpret query results to answer practical, real‑world questions. Demonstrate understanding of basic relational database design Course Prerequisites None.\nRequired Textbooks and Equipment Textbook will be freely available at textbooks.cs.ksu.edu.\nGrading Grading Modality: Graded (A, B, C, D, F, I)\nCourse Schedule Module Topic Homework 1 Relational Database Overview 2 Single Table Queries: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, DISTINCT, LIMIT, OFFSET, CASE, variables, aggregate functions 1 - Single Table Queries 3 Joins: Cross, Inner, Outer 2 - Joins 4 Subqueries 3 - Subqueries 5 Table Expressions and Programmable Objects 4 - Table Expressions and Programmable Objects 6 Database Design 7 Data Modification 8 Final SQL Lab Final Exam/Practical Assignmetns Overview This course will consist of primarily lab exercises that will be completed during in-class labs, along with some take-home practice assignments. The course will conclude with an in-class practical to showcase understanding of the material.\nLabs: 60% Homework: 20% Final Practical: 20%. Proposed Changes Query optimization (indexes, query plans, full-table-scan vs. indexed-lookup cost). thread-algorithmic.md (Algorithmic Thinking \u0026 Complexity spiral) names this course as the pass where complexity reasoning applies to database queries — students read a query plan and see the query planner doing the complexity reasoning for them. Not in this course’s current 8-module schedule, which is query-writing only (SELECT/JOIN/subqueries/design/modification). Proposed as an addition to Module 6 (Database Design) or as a new closing module, pending a course-designer/faculty decision on whether a 1-credit intro-SQL course has room for it. Transactional rollback as a failure model. thread-computational-models.md (Computational Models spiral) names this course’s DECLARATIVE QUERYING pass as also covering transactional rollback — the same “state what, not how” idea as HTML/CSS, now applied to a failure model for data. Not in this course’s current schedule or SLOs (which stop at INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, no transactions/ACID/rollback content). Proposed as an addition alongside Module 7 (Data Modification), same density caveat as the query-optimization item above. Documentation and version control (Professional Practices lens). Confirmed design intent from the 2026-07-13 professional-practice/sociotechnical re-homing table: this course carries schema-decision documentation (“why this schema, not that one”) and solo feature-branching git practice (the escalation step between CIS 116’s read-only git use and CIS 400’s collaborative git). Not present in the current schedule or SLOs (which are pure SQL: querying, joins, design, modification). Proposed alongside Module 6 (Database Design), same density caveat as above. Access control and data minimization as schema decisions (Trustworthy Computing lens). Also confirmed in the same 2026-07-13 re-homing table: data minimization as a schema decision, and access control (“who sees what”) as a property of the schema itself. Not present in the current schedule or SLOs. Proposed alongside Module 6 (Database Design), same density caveat as above. This course now carries four logged proposed additions, covering five distinct findings (query optimization, transactional rollback, schema-decision documentation, solo git branching, access control/data minimization) against an 8-module, 1-credit schedule — the design log’s earlier flag that this course needs a dedicated consolidation pass (rather than more items added one at a time) applies more than ever.",
    "description": "Course Description This 1‑credit hour course introduces students to Structured Query Language (SQL) for retrieving and analyzing data in relational databases. Students will learn to write basic queries to select, filter, summarize, and combine data from multiple tables using hands‑on exercises and real‑world examples.\nCredit Hours 1 credit\nCourse Overview The objective of this course is to provide students with the foundations to understand simple relational databases. With this knowledge, students will be able to understand and write SQL queries to find, organize, and summarize information from real-world datasets.",
    "tags": [],
    "title": "CIS 260 - Foundations of Relational Databases",
    "uri": "/two-year-refactor/2-catalog/courses/cis-260/index.html"
