    "breadcrumb": "Catalog \u003e Courses",
    "content": "Potential new course to be developed. Initial offering would focus on vue as a component-based UX library for the web. Serves as part of a three-course progression, CIS 120 → CIS 220 → CIS 320. May need to be merged with CIS 220 to minimize staffing requirements.\nCourse Description Building user-facing interfaces using a component-based architecture, consuming data from a provided service layer, with an introduction to validating a design against user needs. Sections focus on a specific platform (e.g. web, mobile, desktop); consult the section syllabus for the platform and tools used. (Proposed catalog description, per resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md.)\nCredit Hours 1 credit (per resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md)\nCourse Overview Reframed 2026-07-15 — generic catalog identity, specific curriculum design. Same treatment as CIS 220: catalog identity is platform-agnostic; the two-year core runs the Web section. This is CIS 300’s deliberate pair: same client/server system boundary, opposite side — this course is the client (consuming APIs from a provided/external server), CIS 300 is the server. Deepens CIS 120’s HTML/CSS/JS seed with a component-based UI library (not yet chosen). Includes one bounded exposure to acceptance-criteria/A-B-testing process work — a full deep dive is planned as a Software Architecture specialization elective instead. See resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md’s CIS 320 entry for full detail.\nCourse Goals and Learning Outcomes Proposed catalog learning outcomes (generic, per cs.md): build a user interface from reusable, composable components; consume data from a provided service/API layer to populate and update an interface; apply accessibility and usability standards to an interface design; evaluate a design against a stated set of acceptance criteria. Web-section-specific outcomes not yet drafted.\nCourse Prerequisites Prerequisites: CIS 120, CIS 200, and CIS 220 (per resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md).\nProposed Changes Honest data visualization. thread-human-centered.md (Human-Centered Computing spiral) names this course as the REPRESENT pass — representing data for humans, alongside its confirmed acceptance-criteria/A-B-testing content. Not in this course’s drafted content yet; proposed to ride alongside the component-based UI work, consuming data from the CIS 300 service layer and rendering it responsibly (e.g. avoiding misleading chart scales/framing). Needs a course-designer/faculty decision on scope, given this course’s existing density (acceptance-criteria work already confirmed here on a 1-credit course). Multi-source integration: re-identification risk from combined datasets (Trustworthy Computing lens) and multi-objective tradeoff reasoning (Optimization lens). Same finding as cis-220.md’s matching entry — both lenses describe the CIS 220/CIS 320 pairing carrying a multi-source-integration scenario with no confirmed course to carry it — not this course’s real drafted content (component-based UI consuming a single provided service/API layer). Logged here alongside CIS 220’s entry pending the same course-designer/faculty decision.",
    "description": "Potential new course to be developed. Initial offering would focus on vue as a component-based UX library for the web. Serves as part of a three-course progression, CIS 120 → CIS 220 → CIS 320. May need to be merged with CIS 220 to minimize staffing requirements.\nCourse Description Building user-facing interfaces using a component-based architecture, consuming data from a provided service layer, with an introduction to validating a design against user needs. Sections focus on a specific platform (e.g. web, mobile, desktop); consult the section syllabus for the platform and tools used. (Proposed catalog description, per resources/reference/degree-maps/cs.md.)",
